# C16 (spec 2.1). Sign in with auth.http first: the HTTP client keeps the access cookie.
# Needs transformations@convert (the `user` role has it). Results arrive as attachments.

### The twelve directions: 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/convert/formats

### CSV → JSON: an array of objects; values stay strings
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/convert
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=b

--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="targetFormat"

json
--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="people.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

name,city
Ada,"Zürich, CH"
"""Bob""",Москва
--b--

### JSON → XML: a single-key object is the root; @attributes, #text, arrays repeat
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/convert
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=b

--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="targetFormat"

xml
--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="order.json"
Content-Type: application/json

{"order": {"@id": "7", "item": ["a", "b"], "note": {"@lang": "en", "#text": "hi"}}}
--b--

### XML with a DTD: 400 invalid_file (no DTD, no external entities)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/convert
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=b

--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="targetFormat"

json
--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="xxe.xml"
Content-Type: application/xml

<!DOCTYPE a [<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">]><a>&x;</a>
--b--

### Same format as the source: 400
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/convert
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=b

--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="targetFormat"

csv
--b
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="people.csv"
Content-Type: text/csv

a
1
--b--
